{
  "title": "Covalent Radius",
  "topic": "Covalent Radius",
  "turns": [
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0001",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "Please teach me about covalent radius\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0002",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "Covalent Radius\nLet's learn about covalent radius in a very simple way, with examples! This is an important concept in chemistry that helps us understand the size of an atom.\n1. What is covalent radius?\nWhen two atoms of the same element are joined by a covalent bond, half the distance between their nuclei is called the covalent radius.\nCaptured image\n\nIn an H₂ molecule, the distance between the nuclei of the two hydrogen atoms is 74 pm.\n2. Formula\nr=d2r=2d​​\nWhere:\nrr = covalent radius\n\n\n\ndd = distance between the two nuclei\n\n\n\n\n3. Example\nIn a hydrogen molecule (H₂), if the distance between the two nuclei is 74 pm,\nr=742=37 pmr=274​=37 pm​\nTherefore, the covalent radius of hydrogen is 37 pm.\n4. Key points to remember\nWhen two atoms of the same element are bonded, the covalent radius is half the bond length between them.\nAlso, in general:\nMoving from left to right across the periodic table, atomic radius decreases.\n\n\n\nMoving from top to bottom down a group, atomic radius increases.\n\n\n\n\n5. Now, a short exercise!\nIn a chlorine molecule (Cl₂), the distance between the two nuclei is 198 pm.\nWhat is the covalent radius of chlorine?\n198 pm\n99 pm\n396 pm\n100 pm\n\n\nOne question: Are you studying covalent radius for 11th or 12th grade chemistry? I'll explain it in more detail accordingly.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0003",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "How is the nucleus identified?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0004",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "How is the nucleus identified?\nThe part at the center of an atom that contains protons and neutrons is called the nucleus.\nAtomic Structure - UCalgary Chemistry Textbook\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\nchem-textbook.ucalgary.ca\n\n\n1. How do you identify the nucleus?\nWhen looking at a diagram of an atom:\nProton (p⁺) — has a positive charge.\n\n\n\nNeutron (n⁰) — has no charge.\n\n\n\nElectron (e⁻) — has a negative charge.\n\n\n\n\nProtons and neutrons are found at the center of the atom. That central part is the nucleus.\n2. Where is the nucleus in covalent radius?\nCaptured image\n\nHere, the orange dots represent the nuclei of the two atoms.\nDistance between the two nuclei = 74 pm.\nCovalent radius = 74 ÷ 2 = 37 pm.\n3. A short question\nWhich particles are found in the nucleus?\nElectrons only\nProtons and neutrons\nProtons and electrons\n\n\n\nAn important point: the nucleus cannot be seen directly with an ordinary microscope. Scientists discovered its existence and structure through experiments. In particular, Rutherford's gold foil experiment helped discover the nucleus.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0005",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "How do they measure it?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0006",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "Great question! 😊\nHow do they measure the distance between the nuclei of two atoms? Let's look at it simply.\n1. Can the distance between nuclei be measured directly?\nYou cannot use an ordinary ruler to measure the distance between nuclei.\nThat is because atoms are extremely small.\nSo scientists use methods such as X-ray diffraction.\n2. How do they measure it using X-rays?\nCrystallography: Atomic secrets | Nature\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\nnature.com\n\n\nHere is how it works:\nThey direct X-rays at a crystal.\n\n\n\nThe atoms in the crystal scatter the X-rays.\n\n\n\nThey record the diffraction pattern formed by the scattered rays.\n\n\n\nThey analyze that pattern mathematically to determine the arrangement of the atoms and the distances between them.\n\n\n\n\nThis lets them calculate the covalent bond length.\n3. How do they calculate covalent radius?\nFor example, if an experiment finds that the distance between the nuclei in a chlorine (Cl₂) molecule is 198 pm,\nr=1982=99 pmr=2198​=99 pm​\nThis is the covalent radius of chlorine.\nImportant note: X-ray diffraction is generally used for crystals. To determine bond lengths in gaseous molecules such as H₂ and Cl₂, methods such as rotational spectroscopy and gas electron diffraction are also used.\n4. A question for you to think about\nIf scientists find that the distance between two nuclei is 200 pm, and assuming that the two atoms belong to the same element, what is the covalent radius of each atom?\n200 pm\n100 pm\n400 pm\n\n\nIf you would like, I can explain how X-rays reveal the distances between atoms with an even simpler example.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    }
